refentry.refsect1:
&reftitle.description;
public boolReflectionProperty::isReadable stringnullscope objectnullobject&null;
Checks whether reading the property from the given scope would be allowed. The visibility of the property, its hooks, the __get() and __isset() magic methods and, if an object is given, the state of the property on that object are taken into account.
A return value of &true; does not guarantee that reading the property succeeds. For example, a get hook or __get() may still throw an exception.

&reftitle.returnvalues;
Returns &false; if the property is not visible from scope, if it is a virtual property without a get hook, or if it is uninitialized. Otherwise &true; is returned.
However, if the class defines __get(), &true; is returned for a non-static property that is not visible from scope or does not exist on object. If object is given and the class also defines __isset(), the result of calling __isset() is returned instead.

&reftitle.errors;
Throws a ReflectionException if object is given for a static property, or if object is not an instance of the class the property was declared in.
Throws an Error if scope is not the name of an existing class.

  &reftitle.examples;

   ReflectionProperty::isReadable example


<?php
class User
{
    public string $name;
    private string $password = 'secret';
}

$user = new User();

$nameProperty = new ReflectionProperty(User::class, 'name');

// Without an object, only the declaration of the property is considered
var_dump($nameProperty->isReadable(null));

// The property is not initialized yet
var_dump($nameProperty->isReadable(null, $user));

$user->name = 'Alice';
var_dump($nameProperty->isReadable(null, $user));

$passwordProperty = new ReflectionProperty(User::class, 'password');

// Private properties can only be read from inside the class
var_dump($passwordProperty->isReadable(null));
var_dump($passwordProperty->isReadable(User::class));


   &example.outputs;


bool(true)
bool(false)
bool(true)
bool(false)
bool(true)
